{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint", "file": "references/heterogeneous-demo-reconciliation.md", "content": "# Reconcile heterogeneous or seemingly contradictory demos

With costly demos that vary in size, palette, and visual structure, compare each input/output pair at the exact changed-cell level before choosing an action. Record preserved cells/regions and unchanged pairs as constraints. Normalize semantic color roles only where the edits support the mapping; superficial resemblance to a known task or a remembered reference is not validation.

Test a proposed shared rule against every example, separating trigger, selected extent, and replacement mapping. If some pairs appear incompatible, state that uncertainty instead of ignoring inconvenient examples. After a rejected submission, reject the whole compound hypothesis and rebuild the evidence ledger; do not switch transformation families based solely on unrelated skill notes or make unsupported local edits. Spend another demo only when explicit surviving alternatives predict materially different test outputs and the information justifies its cost. Maintain the required single-action JSON protocol.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint/references/heterogeneous-demo-reconciliation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/noisy-motif-review.md", "content": "# Noisy motif transformation review

For a sparse colored motif embedded in a noisy binary field, record the exact changed-cell mask for every demonstration before proposing a rule. Distinguish seed motif cells, random background coincidences, and newly transformed cells. Check all apparent translated motif placements and treat untransformed near-matches as negative evidence; do not infer template-copying merely because some scattered locations resemble the seed. Compare complete masks across demos, including preserved cells and dimensions.

After an incorrect submission, discard the complete mechanism rather than adjusting only a palette mapping, phase, or a few cells. Rebuild the candidate set from all demonstrations and choose only a materially distinct rule whose trigger, extent, and exceptions explain every observed change. If evidence remains underdetermined, be candid internally about that uncertainty and spend the remaining attempt on the most demo-consistent whole-grid prediction, not a sequence of cosmetic variants.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-checkpoint/references/noisy-motif-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/noisy-grid-connectivity-audit.md", "content": "# Noisy-grid connectivity candidates

For a noisy two-color field containing a small third-color motif, do not assume the motif triggers ordinary flood fill. Compare exact changed masks across all demonstrations and record untouched background cells and preserved marker-colored cells. Test 4-neighbor and 8-neighbor connectivity separately; check whether the marker itself is a seed, a barrier, or merely a pattern that identifies a selected region. Distinguish global connected-component recoloring from localized object/pattern overlays. A large accessible component is not evidence for filling unless the demos show its extent consistently. After a wrong submission, reject the complete candidate and avoid arbitrary boundary/color variants; only advance a materially different rule that explains all observed changed and unchanged cells.
