{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/stripe-sequence-validation.md", "content": "# Uniform stripe sequence tasks: validation and revision

For grids made of full-width, single-color rows, collapse each grid to a 1-D sequence of row colors before inferring the transformation. Compare input and output sequences position by position, including leading and trailing background rows and every gap between visible stripes. Check whether output preserves anchor rows, repeats a motif, extends a sequence, or uses a phase determined by the first/last marker. Do not infer a 2-D periodic tiling merely because other tasks in the broader benchmark use diagonal traces; select the transformation family supported by the current demos.

After a wrong answer, binary feedback rejects the whole candidate but gives no localization. Re-evaluate the rule itself against all demos rather than making repeated near-duplicate grids. Before serializing, validate the output dimensions, count every row's width, and ensure all cell values are valid integers 0–9.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/stripe-sequence-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/sparse-stroke-mapping.md", "content": "# Sparse stroke mapping after mixed demos and rejection

When a sparse grid's output extends colored lines across many background cells, derive the operation from exact coordinates, not visual resemblance. For each demo, record the changed cells by row and column, identify endpoints and seed colors, and separately classify vertical additions, horizontal additions, and any recoloring of existing cells. Then test each candidate rule against every demo, including untouched markers that could look eligible.

Do not combine distinct line effects into one projection merely because the output contains several strokes. In the reviewed instance, demonstrations showed horizontal and vertical continuations, but the submissions combined incompatible inferred continuations and were rejected. This underscores that the trigger, direction, span, and replacement color each need evidence; a plausible-looking set of completed lines is not validated. After binary rejection, rebuild the mapping from all examples and request the remaining demo only if live geometric alternatives predict meaningfully different test grids.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-checkpoint/references/sparse-stroke-mapping.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/seed-expansion-phase-audit.md", "content": "# Seed expansion: phase and payload audit

For a lone non-background cell in a 1×N input that expands to N×N, derive the full output in stages rather than guessing a familiar template. Track each output color independently with exact row/column coordinates across every available size. Fit marker-generated arms, later periodic bands, transition row, phase, clipping, and overlap precedence; verify every demo cell before extrapolating. A pattern that matches the obvious diagonal phase may still fail because secondary-color onset or boundary behavior depends on N.

After a wrong expansion submission, reconsider the full mapping, not merely a phase shift. Distinguish semantic incorrectness from invalid-grid feedback: invalid shape requires mechanical checks that there are exactly N rows, each of width N, with values 0–9. This session's 1×13, 1×5, and 1×7 demos all expanded to squares with outward 2 diagonals and later 1 patterns; repeated width-17 candidates were rejected, so these examples did not establish a validated extrapolation. Treat that precise transition/phase as unresolved rather than canonizing a speculative template.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/seed-expansion-phase-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/cached-reference-provenance.md", "content": "# Verify cached ARC lesson provenance

Skill references are reusable hypotheses, not authoritative evidence about the active instance. Some notes capture a different puzzle, palette, or an attempted rule that was itself rejected. Before applying one, compare its claimed pattern against every demonstration in the current conversation. If it does not account for changed and preserved cells across those pairs, do not use it to justify a submission.

After repeated incorrect submissions, binary feedback rejects the entire candidate without locating the error. Stop speculative retries: rebuild a changed/preserved-cell ledger from all demos, identify assumptions unsupported by that ledger, and choose a materially revised candidate that fits the evidence. Do not switch to an unrelated transformation family merely because a cached note mentions it; when no remaining demo requests are available, prefer the strongest mechanically supported interpretation over arbitrary variants.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/cached-reference-provenance.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/active-reconciliation-after-demo.md", "content": "# Active reconciliation after every demo

A demo request is not progress by itself; the returned pair must be mined before choosing the next action. For each example, compare input and output to locate the exact changed cells and preserved structures, then reconcile those edits with every prior pair. State the current rule candidates and how each predicts the active test. Before buying another demo, name the specific ambiguity it could resolve and ensure candidate explanations imply different observations or test outputs. Do not send consecutive generic requests merely because the demo cap remains. If the demonstrations are heterogeneous or mostly unchanged, treat unchanged regions as counterevidence to a global transformation and identify precisely which regions participate. After rejection, reconsider the whole mapping from the evidence ledger; do not make local cosmetic edits or submit the untouched input absent support. Maintain exactly one protocol-valid JSON action per evaluator turn.
