{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/noisy-background-pattern-propagation.md", "content": "# Noisy-background pattern propagation checkpoint

When a small colored motif is embedded in a mostly two-color noisy field and outputs add that motif color at many scattered locations, do not infer a rule from visual resemblance alone. Compare every input-output pair cell by cell and form the exact changed-cell mask. Test exact motif placements, orientations, overlaps, and near-matches; conspicuous uncolored near-matches are negative evidence that a candidate selection rule must explain. Check that original marker cells are preserved and that all additions—not merely many of them—follow one consistent criterion. Before requesting another demo, state at least two surviving rules, their distinct predictions for the active test, and the concrete example outcome that would discriminate them. A further noisy grid without such a discriminator is not an evidence-led request.

This is a general audit method, not a claim that motif-copying is the right explanation for every noisy grid. See also `noisy-region-hypothesis-audit.md` and `overlapping-pattern-copy-audit.md` for connectivity and false-positive placement pitfalls.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-checkpoint/references/noisy-background-pattern-propagation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/rejected-grid-rule-audit.md", "content": "# Rule audit after a rejected submission

A wrong submission rejects the whole hypothesis, not just one guessed cell. Re-derive from all demo pairs rather than iterating local edits. In cluttered inputs, identify regular object geometry and compare each output cell: isolated cells of other colors within otherwise uniform shapes may be noise or overlays, and the output may normalize the whole object while removing those intrusions. Conversely, do not generalize this without demo support. Audit both the transformed extent and every unusual cell outside it; test the candidate against every demo before spending another attempt.

When a submitted grid is not clearly supported, a further demo can be more valuable than another guess, but specify which competing interpretations it could distinguish. Keep strict protocol: exactly one JSON action per puzzle turn.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/rejected-grid-rule-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/pitfall-after-demo-arrival.md", "content": "# Reconcile before another demo request

In costly sequential ARC interactions, after every demo arrives, compare its changed and preserved cells with all prior pairs before taking another action. State the current candidate rule and a specific uncertainty that another example could settle. Another demo is worth requesting only when at least two plausible rules make distinct predictions on a relevant outcome; otherwise, a generic request burns budget without exploiting the evidence already obtained. Maintain the protocol requirement of exactly one action per puzzle turn.
